# Line endings are LF in the repository AND in the working tree, on every platform.
#
# Not a style preference. Git for Windows ships with core.autocrlf=true in its *system*
# config, so a fresh clone on Windows rewrites every text file to CRLF on checkout. For
# this repo that is a correctness bug, not a cosmetic one:
#
#   - data/*.md is the corpus. CRLF adds one byte per line, so the same document chunks
#     differently on Windows than on Linux - different chunk boundaries, different
#     embeddings, different retrieval results. Every measurement in docs/RUN-LOG.md is
#     implicitly a measurement of the LF corpus.
#   - scripts/*.sh and the Makefile break outright with CRLF (`\r` in a shebang, and tab
#     rules that no longer parse).
#   - ENGINE_MANIFEST.sha256 hashes file contents to detect drift between the three sibling
#     repos. CRLF checkouts change every hash, so parity would report drift that does not
#     exist.
#
# Measured before writing this: cloning this repo on Windows produced acme_10k_excerpt.md
# at 658 bytes with 19 CRLFs, against 639 bytes with 19 LFs in the index.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Files that are fatal rather than merely wrong with CRLF, pinned explicitly so the
# reason survives even if the blanket rule above is ever relaxed.
*.sh    text eol=lf
Makefile text eol=lf
Dockerfile text eol=lf

# Windows-native scripts. PowerShell reads LF fine, and keeping them LF means one rule
# for the whole tree rather than a per-file exception nobody remembers.
*.ps1   text eol=lf
